## Answer

**No**, such a subgroup $Q$ does not always exist. Below is a counterexample.

### Counterexample

Let $G = \text{SL}_2(\mathbb{C})$ and let $T \subset G$ be the diagonal torus
$$T = \left\{ \begin{pmatrix} t & 0 \\ 0 & t^{-1} \end{pmatrix} : t \in \mathbb{C}^\times \right\} \cong \mathbb{G}_m.$$

Consider the normalizer $N := N_G(T)$, which is a closed (hence algebraic) subgroup of $\text{SL}_2(\mathbb{C})$. Explicitly,
$$N = T \;\cup\; wT, \qquad w := \begin{pmatrix} 0 & -1 \\ 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix},$$
where $wT$ consists of all matrices $\begin{pmatrix} 0 & -t \\ t^{-1} & 0 \end{pmatrix}$ for $t \in \mathbb{C}^\times$.

### Identifying $N^\circ$ and $N/N^\circ$

- $T \cong \mathbb{G}_m$ is connected, so $T \subseteq N^\circ$.
- $N = T \sqcup wT$ has exactly two connected components (the coset $wT$ is a translate of $T$, hence connected and disjoint from $T$).
- Therefore $N^\circ = T$, which is normal in $N$ (index 2), and
$$N / N^\circ \cong \mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}.$$

### Every element of $wT$ has order 4

For any $t \in \mathbb{C}^\times$, set $g_t = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & -t \\ t^{-1} & 0 \end{pmatrix} \in wT$. A direct computation gives
$$g_t^2 = \begin{pmatrix} -t \cdot t^{-1} & 0 \\ 0 & -t^{-1} \cdot t \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -1 & 0 \\ 0 & -1 \end{pmatrix} = -I.$$

Since $-I \in T$ has order 2, every $g_t \in wT$ satisfies $g_t^2 = -I \neq I$ and $g_t^4 = I$, so **every element of $N \setminus T$ has order exactly 4**.

### No complement $Q \cong \mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}$ exists

Suppose, for contradiction, that there exists a subgroup $Q \subseteq N$ with $Q \cong N/N^\circ \cong \mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}$ and $N = N^\circ \cdot Q = T \cdot Q$.

Since $|Q| = |N/N^\circ| = 2$ and $|T \cap Q| \cdot |T \cdot Q| = |T| \cdot |Q|$, the condition $N = TQ$ forces $T \cap Q = \{1\}$, so $Q = \{1, q\}$ for some $q \in N \setminus T$ with $q^2 = 1$.

But $N \setminus T = wT$, and we showed every element of $wT$ has order 4, in particular $q^2 = -I \neq I$. This contradicts $q^2 = 1$.

Therefore **no such subgroup $Q$ exists**. $\blacksquare$

### Conclusion

The extension of algebraic groups
$$1 \longrightarrow G^\circ \longrightarrow G \longrightarrow G/G^\circ \longrightarrow 1$$
does **not** always split. The normalizer $N_{\text{SL}_2(\mathbb{C})}(T)$ provides an explicit counterexample: its component group $\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}$ cannot be realized as a subgroup of $N$ complementing $T$, because the non-trivial coset contains no element of order 2 (every element there squares to $-I$, hence has order 4).

### PROOF COMPLETE
